      "body": "Skip to lesson\nr\nrecall\nweave\n● PRIVATE · RUNS ON THIS DEVICE\nFIELD NOTES / LOCAL DECK\nLearn the links,\nnot just the labels.\n\nEdit distance: prefixes, costs and one optimal alignment\n\n↗\n12 challenges\n·\nat your pace\nOperations and costs\nPrefix subproblems\nOptimal reconstruction\nRepresentation and limits\n\nPrerequisite links in your deck guide selection. The same illustrative model estimates each concept; it does not validate the content or assess ability.\n\nBring your own lesson\n\nChoose a local JSON deck to preview its questions. Nothing is uploaded. The current session changes only when you start the selected deck.\n\nChoose a deck file\nDownload example deck\nUse bundled lesson\n\nStarted “Edit distance: prefixes, costs and one optimal alignment”. This lesson stays in this tab until you reload or choose another deck.\n\nTHREAD COMPLETE\n12 / 12\n⌁\nYOUR LEARNING TRACE\nNotice the links you built.\n\nYou made 11 of 12 connections on the first try. That count is a snapshot—not a measure of your ability. Review the concept estimates and choose one connection to explain in your own words.\n\nEstimated mastery · model state, not a grade\nOperations and costs\n90%\nPrefix subproblems\n98%\nOptimal reconstruction\n98%\nRepresentation and limits\n98%\nPractice complete\n\nYou answered 1 of 1 correctly on retry.\n\nYou have seen these explanations. Practice is for recall; your first answers and model estimates stay as they were.\n\nReview the connections\n\nOpen a question to see your first answer and explanation, then write how you would apply the idea.\n\n01 · Operations and costs\nNeeds review · first try\nWith insertion, deletion and substitution each costing 1, what is the minimum cost to turn cat into cut?\n02 · Prefix subproblems\nCorrect · first try\nD[i,j] uses prefix lengths i and j. What does it mean in this lab?\n03 · Operations and costs\nCorrect · first try\nInsertion costs 2 and deletion costs 3. What are the minimum costs of empty → abc and abc → empty, respectively?\n04 · Operations and costs\nCorrect · first try\nFor a → b, insertion and deletion cost 1 each, while substitution costs 4. Which result minimizes cost?\n05 · Prefix subproblems\nCorrect · first try\nFor ab → ac with all edit costs 1, consider D[2,2]. D[1,1]=0, D[1,2]=1 and D[2,1]=1. What are the diagonal, deletion and insertion candidate costs?\n06 · Optimal reconstruction\nCorrect · first try\nWith unit costs, aa → a has minimum cost 1. A deterministic backtracker displays one alignment. What can you conclude?\n07 · Optimal reconstruction\nCorrect · first try\nOnly insert, delete and substitute are allowed, each at cost 1. What is the minimum cost for ab → ba?\n08 · Representation and limits\nCorrect · first try\nThe source has 3 scalar values and the target has 4. How many prefix cells are in the full table, including empty prefixes?\n09 · Representation and limits\nCorrect · first try\nThe lab treats Unicode scalars literally and does not normalize text. The source is one scalar é (U+00E9); the target is e (U+0065) followed by combining acute (U+0301). With unit costs, what is the minimum?\n10 · Prefix subproblems\nCorrect · first try\nWith unit edit costs, why can tack → stack be achieved at cost 1 even though comparing equal screen positions shows several mismatches?\n11 · Optimal reconstruction\nCorrect · first try\nWhich check establishes that a reconstructed alignment actually realizes the reported minimum for the accepted inputs?\n12 · Representation and limits\nCorrect · first try\nA lab experiment reports minimum cost 0 under this positive-cost scalar policy. Which statement follows?\nApply it in your own words\n\nChoose one connection from this deck and explain how it relates to another idea in your own words.\n\nYour writing is not scored. Download study notes to keep it. Starting a fresh session or another deck clears this writing.\n\nAttribution supplied in the deck: Original course, examples and explanations authored for RecallWeave with AI assistance. Conceptual references: Wagner & Fischer, The String-to-String Correction Problem (JACM1974, doi:10.1145/321796.321811), and MIT OpenCourseWare6.006 Spring2020 Recitation16. No source questions, passages or code are copied.\nLicense supplied in the deck: Original lesson content: CC BY 4.0. Reference works retain their own licenses.\n\nKeep these connections\n\nSave your questions, first answers, explanations, reflections, and any practice answers as a text file.\n\nDownload study notes (.txt)\n\nDownload requested. Check your browser’s downloads for the study notes. Your session is still here.\n\nStart a fresh local session\nSave or resume an unfinished lesson\nKeep or restore a learning trace\nHow the adaptive model works\nRECALLWEAVE · DEMO BUILD\nLocal deck · supplied attribution and license appear in the learning trace"
